<p>Dogs belong in family stories, so it makes sense that many couples want them at the wedding. The mistake is treating “included” as “present for the entire day”.</p>
<h2>Choose the moments that matter</h2>
<p>For many dogs, the best wedding role is short and meaningful: getting ready for a few minutes, walking into the ceremony, joining portraits, or appearing during a quiet part of the reception.</p>
<h2>Build a dog timeline inside the wedding timeline</h2>
<p>Write down who has the lead, where the dog rests, when they eat, where they can toilet, how they travel and what happens immediately after their photos. The dog’s plan should not depend on a bridesmaid remembering between speeches.</p>
<h2>Have an exit before you need one</h2>
<p>Even a social dog can become tired by music, unfamiliar people, children, heat or constant attention. A quiet room or private sitter gives them a real break and gives you permission to stay present at your own wedding.</p>
<h2>Wedding Dog Concierge</h2>
<p>Nani Pup can combine dog care with the practical wedding moments: collection, walking, bringing the dog to the agreed portrait or ceremony window, calm handling and returning them to rest afterwards.</p>
<div class="noteBox"><strong>Photographer’s bonus:</strong> when dog care and portrait timing are coordinated, the dog is usually fresher, calmer and much easier to photograph than after hours of waiting.</div>
<p>For Lake Bled weddings, tell us your venue, rough timeline, accommodation and what you want your dog to be part of. The care plan can then be built around those moments.</p>
